@@ -69,6 +69,24 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON document supplied as a string, with syntax highlighting. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON, indented with 4 spaces by default. Pass ``indent`` to change the number of spaces, or ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable can also be passed to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, for example ``console.log(JSON(text))``. The renderable disables word wrapping. Under default printing, long lines are cropped to the console width. Enable soft wrapping (``soft_wrap=True``) to keep each line whole.
+
+The :func:`rich.print_json` function is a shortcut which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich", "version": 10}')
+
+Printing JSON from the command line is also supported. Run the json module as a program with the path to a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
